    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a> withdrew the Muslim forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and gathered them on the Yarmuk plain, which forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> to bring their five armies together too, in a battle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> had wished to avoid.</li><li>For five days <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a> fought mainly on the defensive, using a mounted reserve to counter each Byzantine attack; on the sixth he massed about 8,000 cavalry and drove the Byzantine horse from the field.</li><li>Estimates of the armies differ widely: most early accounts put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> at 60,000 to 70,000 and the Muslims at 36,000 to 40,000, while some modern estimates for the Byzantine army are as low as 15,000 to 20,000.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Early Muslim sources say Gregory's Byzantine infantry were chained together in lengths of ten men as a sign that they would not retreat; modern historians suggest instead that they used the Graeco-Roman testudo formation.</p></aside>
    </section>

