    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">627</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> wins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> is overthrown soon after</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">632</span><span class="rf-text">Muhammad dies, Abu Bakr becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and puts down the Ridda revolts</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">634</span><span class="rf-text">Abu Bakr calls for the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> are beaten at Ajnadayn</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early 636</span><span class="rf-text">Muslim forces take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> prepares a counterattack</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 636</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> launches his offensive and the Byzantine army gathers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 636</span><span class="rf-text">Six days of fighting at the Yarmuk end with the rout of the Byzantine army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">639–642</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> invades and conquers Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-the-yarmuk" data-v="18f269803f" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies stand facing each other</b> The Byzantines faced east and the Muslims west, on fronts of about 13 and 12 kilometres. Each side had four sections, and each Byzantine army kept its cavalry in the rear. On the first day a third of the Byzantine infantry attacked and neither side gained ground.</li><li><b>The Byzantine wings drive the Muslim wings back</b> The Byzantine centre only pinned the Muslim centre. On the wings Qanatir's Slavs and Gregory's army attacked in force, and the Muslim wings fell back towards their camps.</li><li><b>Khalid's guard strikes the Byzantine left wing</b> Khalid sent the mobile guard against the southern flank of the Byzantine left wing while the Muslim right wing attacked from the front. The Byzantine left wing gave up the ground it had won and Amr regained his position.</li><li><b>Qanatir presses the right again and Khalid flanks the centre</b> Qanatir led his Slavs, with help from Armenians and Jabalah's Christian Arabs, against the Muslim right wing and right centre, which fell back. Khalid sent the mobile guard at the flanks of the Byzantine left centre while Abu Ubayda and Yazid attacked on the left.</li><li><b>Dhirar secures the bridge in the night</b> Khalid had grouped all his cavalry into one force of about 8,000, with the mobile guard as its core. He sent Dhirar with 500 cavalry round the Byzantine northern flank to take the bridge at Ayn Dhakar, the best crossing of the western ravine.</li><li><b>The cavalry sweeps round the left and routs the horse</b> Khalid galloped his cavalry round the Byzantine left wing and attacked its rear while the Muslim right wing pressed from the front, and the left wing collapsed. He then wheeled back upon the Byzantine cavalry as it formed and routed it to the north.</li><li><b>The Byzantine centre breaks and the army is trapped</b> Khalid's cavalry fell on the Byzantine left centre from behind and broke it, and a general retreat began. The Byzantines fell back west towards the bridge, which Dhirar's men already held, while Khalid took his cavalry north to block that way out.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heraclius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heraclius.jpg" alt="Heraclius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraclius_solidus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> became emperor in 610, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-sasanian-war-of-602-628">Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 611 and went on to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Heraclius launched his offensive in 622 and won a victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 627. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> was then overthrown and killed in a coup led by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>, who sued for peace and agreed to withdraw from all occupied Byzantine territory. In 629 Heraclius restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><p>Meanwhile Muhammad had preached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and by 630 had brought most of it under a single political authority. After his death in June 632 Abu Bakr was chosen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and defeated rebel tribes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ridda-wars">Ridda Wars</a> of 632–633. He then began wars of conquest, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>, and in February 634 issued a call to arms for the invasion of Syria. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> were beaten at Ajnadayn in July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> fell in September, and the last significant garrison of Palestine was routed at Fahl.</p><p>Abu Bakr's successor Umar wished to carry the conquest deeper into Syria. He replaced Khalid with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ubayda-ibn-al-jarrah">Abu Ubayda ibn al-Jarrah</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalbek">Baalbek</a> fell without much struggle, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> early in 636. Heraclius, alarmed, prepared to win back the lost regions. In 635 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> sought an alliance and was to attack in Iraq at the same time, but he could not coordinate with Heraclius's offensive of May 636, probably because his government was exhausted.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Yarmuk&#8221; cites where it covers Background:</p><ul><li>Geoffrey Greatrex, The Roman Eastern Frontier and the Persian Wars (Part II, 363–628 AD) (Routledge, 2002)</li><li>John Haldon, Byzantium in the Seventh Century: the Transformation of a Culture (Cambridge University Press, 1997)</li><li>David Nicolle, Yarmuk 636 A.D.: The Muslim Conquest of Syria (Osprey Publishing, 1994)</li><li>A. I. Akram, The Sword of Allah: Khalid bin al-Waleed – His Life and Campaigns (Oxford University Press, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By May 636 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> had gathered a large force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, with contingents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a>, organised in five armies. Theodore Trithyrius was their joint leader and Vahan, an Armenian and former garrison commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, was overall field commander, with an Armenian army. Qanatir, a Slavic prince, led the Slavs, Jabalah ibn al-Aiham, king of the Ghassanid Arabs, a Christian Arab force, and Gregory and Dairjan the remaining contingents. Heraclius supervised from Antioch and did not wish to fight one pitched battle. He planned to attack the divided Muslim corps one by one.</p><p>The Muslims learned of this from Byzantine prisoners. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a>'s advice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-ubayda-ibn-al-jarrah">Abu Ubayda</a> gathered the whole army in one place, and after raids and the risk of an attack on the rear it moved to the eastern plain of the Yarmuk. In a council of war Abu Ubayda gave command to Khalid, who formed 36 infantry regiments and four cavalry regiments and kept his elite mobile guard in reserve. The line was 12 kilometres long and faced west. The Byzantine line, about 13 kilometres long and facing east, had gaps between its divisions, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> stood behind the infantry.</p><p>Most early accounts give the Muslims 36,000 to 40,000 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> 60,000 to 70,000. Some modern estimates put the Byzantine army at 40,000 at most, others at 15,000 to 20,000, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> army at 15,000 to 40,000, most likely around 36,000. Warren Treadgold suggests 80,000 to 100,000 or more for the Byzantines, while acknowledging the uncertainty.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Both armies were in position in late July, and for about a month there was only a skirmish and talks. The fighting began on 15 August. Champions from each side duelled, and Vahan sent a third of his infantry in a limited attack at midday, which lacked determination. Muslim chronicles record that George, a Byzantine unit commander, rode over to the Muslim line, became a Muslim and died fighting on that side the same day. On 16 August Vahan attacked at dawn, hoping to catch the Muslims at morning prayers, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a> had set outposts. Qanatir's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> pushed back the Muslim right and Yazid's left wing was driven back to its camps, but Khalid's mobile guard restored both flanks. Muslim women in the camps, led by Hind, are said to have turned the retreating men back, and Dairjan was killed.</p><p>On 17 and 18 August Vahan pressed the Muslim right and right centre, which fell back twice and were rallied, and each time Khalid struck the Byzantine flanks with his mobile guard. On the fourth day Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> wounded many Muslims in the eyes, so that it became known as the Day of Lost Eyes. Ikrimah's 400 cavalry covered the Muslim retreat, and all were killed or badly wounded. Ikrimah died that evening.</p><p>On 19 August Vahan asked for a truce, and Khalid refused. He massed about 8,000 horsemen and sent Dhirar with 500 cavalry by night to take the bridge at Ayn Dhakar. On 20 August the Muslim cavalry rode round the Byzantine left, routed the Byzantine horse to the north, and attacked the infantry from the rear. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> fell back west towards the ravine of Wadi-ur-Ruqqad, where Dhirar already held the bridge. Some fell into the ravines or were killed in flight, and many escaped.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Yarmuk&#8221; cites where it covers Byzantine counterattack, Battlefield, Battle, Day 1:</p><ul><li>A. I. Akram, The Sword of Allah: Khalid bin al-Waleed – His Life and Campaigns (Oxford University Press, 2004)</li><li>David Nicolle, Yarmuk 636 A.D.: The Muslim Conquest of Syria (Osprey Publishing, 1994)</li><li>Walter Emil Kaegi, Byzantium and the Early Islamic Conquests (Cambridge University Press, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/true-cross"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/true-cross.jpg" alt="True Cross" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_Crucifixion.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giotto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theodore Trithyrius was killed on the field. Niketas escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, and Jabalah escaped, briefly came to terms with the Muslims and then went back to the Byzantine court. The Muslims took no prisoners in the battle but may have captured some in the pursuit. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid</a> and his mobile guard went north after the survivors, found them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and attacked. Vahan, who had escaped the field, was probably killed there. Khalid then entered Damascus, where the residents welcomed him.</p><p>When the news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> he was devastated and enraged. He blamed his own wrongdoings, above all his marriage to his niece Martina. He no longer had the men or the money to defend the province. He held a service of intercession in the cathedral of Antioch, where his advisers told him almost unanimously, and he accepted, that the defeat was God's decision and the result of the sins of the people of the land, himself among them. He then sailed by night to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Sophronius, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, is said to have secretly put the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> and other relics on a ship to protect them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>.</p><p>Heraclius turned to the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> fell to the Muslims in 638–639, and he created a buffer zone in central Anatolia by ordering all the forts east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> to be evacuated. In 639–642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>, who had commanded the Muslim right at the Yarmuk, invaded and captured Byzantine Egypt.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of the Yarmuk&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Walter Emil Kaegi, Byzantium and the Early Islamic Conquests (Cambridge University Press, 1995)</li><li>Geoffrey Regan, First Crusader: Byzantium's Holy Wars (Palgrave Macmillan, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor.jpg" alt="Theophanes the Confessor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes the Confessor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athonite_Icon_of_Saint_Theophanes_the_Confessor_of_Megalos_Agros_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tzortzis Phouka, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Most early accounts are Arab, and they generally agree that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and their allies outnumbered the Muslims two to one. The only early Byzantine source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor">Theophanes</a>, who wrote a century later. Accounts disagree on the length of the battle, some giving a day and others six. Several details come from Muslim chronicles and tradition: George's conversion, the verse sung by the women, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>'s farewell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and the chains of Gregory's infantry, which modern historians read instead as a testudo. Greek court sources accused Vahan of treason for disobeying Heraclius's order to avoid a large battle.</p><p>The size of the armies is the main dispute. One estimate of the Byzantine army, 60,000 to 70,000, was reached by considering the supply and the weakened state of the empire after 628, while Treadgold argues from the empire's fiscal capacity and pay records for far more. Other scholars point to logistical limits and give no figure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle ended Byzantine rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and was the culmination of the first great wave of early Muslim conquests after Muhammad's death, which carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> into both the Sasanian and Byzantine empires. It is regarded as one of the most decisive battles in military history. It was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s greatest victory and made his reputation as a tactician and cavalry commander.</p><p>Assessments of the Byzantine side stress its failures. Its commanders let the enemy choose the field, held their cavalry in static reserve for most of the six days, and never concentrated their numbers for a breakthrough, even when they gained one on the fourth day. The lack of resolve may have come from the difficulty of commanding an army divided by internal conflict, and Vahan's numbers may have been of little use in the terrain. Khalid, in this reading, carried out on the field on a small scale what <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a> had planned on a grand scale, defeating a larger force in parts by moving quickly.</p><p>George F. Nafziger, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> at War, calls the Yarmuk one of the most decisive battles in human history and argues that a Byzantine victory would have left the modern world unrecognisable.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>In August 636 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-ibn-al-walid">Khalid ibn al-Walid</a>'s Muslim army fought six days at the Yarmuk against a Byzantine army that most early accounts put at about twice its size, and on the last day routed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> fell within six years.</em></p>

